    "agent_last_message": "I've written the tool as a single file, `output/main.js`, using only the Node standard library. It runs as `node main.js --input <file> --output <file>`. I checked every edge case listed in the spec, and the output matched an independent Python count exactly on a 156 MB file. That file took about 5 seconds.\n\n**How it works:**\n- **Large files:** it reads the file in 4 MB chunks rather than loading it all at once. Words and multi-byte characters that fall across a chunk boundary are handled correctly; the 156 MB comparison was built to test that. A 60 MB file containing a single word also works.\n- **Counts:** \"characters\" counts bytes, as the spec says. A last line without a trailing newline still counts as a line.\n- **Words:** only the six whitespace characters listed in the spec split words. Lowercasing handles non-English letters too (`\u00c9` becomes `\u00e9`).\n- **Tie order:** words with the same count are sorted by Unicode code point, which keeps emoji and other characters outside the Basic Multilingual Plane in the right place.\n- **Arguments:** both `--input x` and `--input=x` work.\n\n**Exit codes:**\n- **1** for a missing, unknown, duplicated or empty argument; an input file that is missing, a directory, or unreadable; and an output file that can't be written. The spec doesn't say which code that last case should get, so I chose 1.\n- **2** for any other unexpected error. One limit: if Node itself runs out of memory, the process aborts with its own code and can't return 2.\n\n**Decision for you:** I treat a file that isn't valid UTF-8 text as \"malformed\" and exit with 1. The spec doesn't define \"malformed\", and this matches what a typical Python or Rust version would do when reading the file as text. If you'd rather accept any bytes and still produce counts, it's a small change.\n\nThere's also a byte-order mark (BOM) detail: if a file starts with one, it stays attached to the first word rather than being stripped. That follows from the spec counting every byte, but it means a",
